{"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/documentation\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/OrientedIncidenceMatrix","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/documentation\/IncidenceMatrixModule"]]},"metadata":{"fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"OrientedIncidenceMatrix","kind":"identifier"}],"modules":[{"name":"IncidenceMatrixModule"}],"roleHeading":"Structure","externalID":"s:21IncidenceMatrixModule08OrientedaB0V","role":"symbol","title":"OrientedIncidenceMatrix","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"OrientedIncidenceMatrix"}],"remoteSource":{"fileName":"OrientedIncidenceMatrix.swift","url":"https:\/\/github.com\/Tyler-Keith-Thompson\/Grafluent\/blob\/main\/Sources\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/OrientedIncidenceMatrix.swift#L25"},"symbolKind":"struct"},"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The oriented incidence matrix of a directed hypergraph. Entry (v, p) is −1 when "},{"code":"v","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" is a source"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"of column "},{"code":"p","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", +1 when it is a target, and 0 otherwise."}],"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"platforms":["Linux"],"tokens":[{"kind":"attribute","text":"@frozen"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"OrientedIncidenceMatrix"}],"languages":["swift"]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"content":[{"type":"heading","text":"Overview","anchor":"overview","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"This is the matrix of Gallo, Longo, Pallottino and Nguyen (1993). A directed graph’s edge is a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"column with one source and one target, which gives the oriented incidence matrix of spectral"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"graph theory (B·Bᵀ = L). The matrix conforms to "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"DirectedHypergraph"},{"type":"text","text":", so the "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Hypergraphs"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"algorithms (B- and F-connection, components, the clique expansion) run on it unchanged. It is"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"not a "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"DirectedGraph"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"heading","text":"Two bit planes","anchor":"Two-bit-planes","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The sources and the targets are each stored as an "},{"code":"IncidenceMatrix","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", so a vertex can be on both"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"sides of a column. Its entry is then target − source = 0, with both bits kept."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"sources(ofHyperedgeAt:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"code":"targets(ofHyperedgeAt:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", the stars and equality read the planes,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"so nothing is lost. A directed self-loop is such a column. "},{"code":"reversed","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" swaps the planes, negating"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"every entry, and "},{"code":"transposed()","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" transposes each plane to give the dual. Both are O(1)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Storage is 4·n·m\/8 bytes, so the matrix suits dense or small incidence. ","type":"text"},{"code":"IncidenceList","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"sparse representation. Equality compares both planes cell for cell, with equal shape, so column","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"order counts.","type":"text"}]}],"kind":"content"}],"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/incidencematrix